it took nearly 20 years of parliamentary  debates and concessions to commercialize  nigeria's oil company  the government hopes the new firm will  attract private investors and cut waste  and corruption  we are transforming our petroleum  industry  to strengthen its capacity  and market the relevance  for the present and future global energy  priorities  leading the transformation is an  industry veteran  this company can make quick decisions  can invest quickly can borrow quickly  can pay back loans when they do and also  but most importantly to be accountable  to shareholders  the oil and gas sector reforms come as  global attention move towards cleaner  energy  with proven reserves of 206 trillion  cubic feet of gas and hoping to grow  that to 600 trillion  official expectations here  are understandably high  for a country  so blessed in gas like ours  we cannot  be happier  and  more excited  but concerns remain  many here are worried that the era of  cheap oil they are used to will soon be  over  in the absence of these  workers or refineries we import this  thing  and  if you import it  hide and  and to be honest there's a limit i don't  see i don't see how government can  continue with this subsidy in nigeria  at about 40 cents per liter nigerians  pay less for petrol than almost anyone  else in the world critics say subsidies  have stifled growth and bred corruption  now with private investors coming in  nigerians may have to pay more for fuel  the new company wants to offer shares  for sale as early as next year it's hard  to say whether this commercial venture  will go the way previous privatized  companies would struggle with assets  tripping and corruption or will prove to  be an exception  muhammad idris al-jazeera abuja nigeria
 

